module bindings.java.output_stream_slice_write_bounds;

// java.io.OutputStream's array-slice transfer contract puts its bounds
// obligation in writing: write(byte[] b, int off, int len) throws
// IndexOutOfBoundsException "if off is negative, len is negative, or len is
// greater than b.length - off" -- and the same three conditions are restated
// by InputStream.read, Reader.read and Writer.write for their array-slice
// overloads. An implementation that compares off + len at int width cannot
// discharge that contract: a pair large enough to wrap passes as a negative
// sum, which is exactly where java.io.FileOutputStream's own check stood
// before the wrap was rejected explicitly, and the bytes then leave at an
// offset the array never had.
//
// The shape labelled here is the delegating override that has taken that
// contract over only partway. It is a write on an array of bytes or chars
// with the slice arguments (three or more parameters), it hands the raw off
// and len to another write -- super.write, or a wrapped stream's write --
// and its own body makes a comparison, so it is in the validation business
// at this call rather than forwarding blind. What it never does is mention
// the array's length: no condition in the method bounds off or len against
// b.length, in the overflow-safe spelling or any other. A pass-through
// override with no comparison of its own is not reported: it forwards the
// contract whole, and the stream it forwards to owns the checks -- the
// finding for a wrapping check belongs to that stream's write, not to the
// transparent wrapper.
//
// Whether the pair is attacker-chosen is the rule's question, not this
// binding's. Robocode's host hands these objects to robots it does not
// trust, a log framework's wrapper answers to whoever opened the stream:
// the label says the bounds contract was partially claimed and never
// completed, and the review weighs who can choose the pair.
//
// Two deliberate residuals. A comparison in the overflow-prone spelling
// (off + len > b.length) mentions the length and is not reported, although
// that spelling is itself the narrow-width variant -- the Go binding for the
// same concept labels exactly that arithmetic where the frontend can see the
// widths, and chasing every Java spelling of the sum would key on variable
// names rather than the contract. And a bounds check extracted into a helper
// leaves no .length in the override, so a delegated check cannot clear the
// label -- the known function-local visibility limit for controls, in the
// false-positive direction.
binding outputStreamSliceWriteBounds9450 {
  query pattern presenceNode where node.scope == "function" and node.context.functionName == "write" and node.context.paramType in ["byte", "char"] and node.context.paramIndex == "2" and node.context.call == "write" and containsAny(node.context.binaryShape, ["<", ">", "==", "!="]) and not (node.context.selector contains ".length")
  emit issue code.OffsetLengthBoundsOverflow at node
}
